Version 1.0 • 23 October 2026

 1. Executive Summary
AidFlow is a failure-resilient micro-logistics coordination prototype for surplus cooked food and emergency aid. It connects donors, volunteer riders, and receiving shelters while treating expiry deadlines, carrying capacity, destination compatibility, reservation reliability, and changing ground conditions as active constraints.
The supplied problem statement highlights three operational failure classes: bulk supply does not match rider/shelter capacity; claimed batches can be stranded by unresponsive volunteers; and destination demand can change while cargo is in transit. It also identifies resource dependencies, such as raw ration kits requiring clean water or cooking fuel.
THE DIFFERENTIATOR
Do not pitch AidFlow as another donor directory or AI matching app. Pitch the AidFlow Rescue Protocol: precomputed fallback candidates, a visible rescue clock, cargo-aware recovery, live feasibility revalidation, and an auditable explanation of every decision.

Success definition
•	A judge can create a time-sensitive batch and dispatch it across multiple riders.
•	A ghost reservation expires and releases stock exactly once.
•	A shelter closure or demand reduction triggers a safe reroute or an explicit intervention alert.
•	Unsafe, incompatible, over-capacity, expired, and duplicate assignments are blocked by deterministic code.
•	The app starts from documented commands, persists state, has automated tests, and can be demoed without credentials or internet access.
What this prototype is not
•	Not a production emergency-response system or a substitute for trained logistics/safety personnel.
•	Not real GPS tracking, live traffic prediction, food-safety certification, or medical advice.
•	Not dependent on an LLM for safety-critical allocation decisions.
•	Not a promise of a measured real-world reduction in spoilage; any impact figures must be benchmark results from clearly labeled simulations.
