7. Core User Journeys
Journey A — Create and dispatch surplus
9.	Operator creates a batch with category, quantity, donor origin, supplied expiry deadline, and handling/resource requirements.
10.	AidFlow validates inputs and calculates the current feasible window.
11.	Dispatch preview evaluates riders and shelters and shows accepted/rejected candidates with reasons.
12.	The operator confirms the allocation; the backend atomically reserves quantities and creates assignment records.
13.	Riders acknowledge reservations; the event log records each transition.
Journey B — Ghost reservation
14.	A rider reserves a shipment but does not acknowledge it before the configured lease deadline.
15.	The simulation clock or timeout processor detects the overdue lease.
16.	The reservation expires exactly once; quantity is returned to the correct available pool.
17.	The system searches for a feasible reassignment and records the result.
18.	The UI shows the timeout trigger, quantity released, reassignment or intervention outcome, and event history.
Journey C — Destination changes during transit
19.	A shipment is marked picked up/in transit, with custody assigned to the carrying rider.
20.	The shelter closes or its remaining demand drops.
21.	The system does not release the cargo as donor stock. It marks the original destination as infeasible and evaluates alternatives.
22.	A new destination is selected only after current demand, requirements, capacity, and expiry feasibility pass.
23.	If no safe option exists, the shipment enters an intervention-required state; it is not falsely marked delivered.
8. Dispatch and Recovery Logic
Hard constraints before ranking
•	Batch is active and not past its configured safe-use deadline.
•	Candidate shelter is active and has positive remaining demand for the category.
•	Item category and handling rules are compatible with the shelter.
•	Required resources are available at the destination.
•	Rider is available and remaining carrying capacity is sufficient for the allocated quantity.
•	Estimated arrival plus safety margin is no later than the supplied safe-use deadline.
•	Allocation does not exceed remaining batch quantity, shelter demand, or rider capacity.
•	For in-transit cargo, recovery preserves the existing cargo custody and does not create a duplicate physical shipment.
Simple explainable ranking
After hard constraints are applied, rank feasible candidates with a deterministic weighted score. Keep the formula configurable and explain the values shown to the operator.
score = urgency_weight * urgency + demand_weight * demand_priority - travel_weight * estimated_minutes - risk_weight * uncertainty_penalty
Do not use this score to override a failed hard constraint. A high score cannot make an unsafe or infeasible candidate eligible.
Quantity accounting model
Quantity bucket	Meaning	May be reallocated?
Available / unassigned	Still held at donor or otherwise not committed.	Yes, if still eligible and unexpired.
Reserved	Allocated to a rider but not picked up.	Only after valid cancellation/expiry and exactly-once release.
Picked up / in transit	Physical cargo is under rider custody.	Only through a valid reroute/recovery flow; never as donor-held stock.
Delivered	Confirmed accepted delivery.	No.
Intervention required	Cargo exists but no feasible automated recovery is currently available.	Only through an explicit operator action and revalidation.

State machine
AVAILABLE -> RESERVED -> ACKNOWLEDGED -> PICKED_UP -> IN_TRANSIT -> DELIVERED
Additional transitions include RESERVED -> EXPIRED/CANCELLED -> AVAILABLE, and active shipment -> INTERVENTION_REQUIRED. Model custody separately from assignment status if that avoids ambiguous states. Never expire a shipment reservation after pickup as though the cargo were still at the donor.
Time handling
•	Store timestamps consistently (UTC in persisted records; render local time in the UI).
•	Use an injectable clock for tests and a simulated clock for the judge demo.
•	Use the original configured safe-use deadline; do not extend it during reassignment.
•	Treat estimated travel times as simulated unless connected to a real routing provider.
•	Add a configurable safety margin and explain it in the interface.
